# @system runtime-auth-telegram-connect-ts
# @status generated
# @edit change registry input, then regenerate

# Package-source repo .gitignore.
# Per constitution: bun.lock is a resolution cache in package-source repos,
# NOT a committed deploy artifact (that's service repos only — per
# railway-builds-resolve-fresh). The package-poller and bun install manage
# it; committing it is drift noise. dist/ and build/ are publish artifacts,
# never tracked in source repos.

.codegen-cache.json
.npmrc

# Install state — never committed
.scala-install.lock
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Build outputs
dist/
**/dist/
build/
**/build/
# Packed tarballs. `npm pack` writes <name>-<version>.tgz into the package dir,
# and the publish smoke test packs into a STAGED CHECKOUT OF THE TAG — so a
# committed .tgz reappears there beside the freshly packed one and the smoke
# test can validate the wrong artifact entirely.
# Measured 2026-08-20: tools/base/service-runtime/teamscala-service-runtime-1.15.219.tgz
# was committed on 2026-08-16 and silently smoke-tested in place of every
# candidate for four days. Three publishes in one evening failed with an
# identical error while the tag, the working tree and the package dir were all
# verifiably clean. publish_validation.rs now REFUSES on an ambiguous candidate;
# this stops the ambiguity being creatable in the first place
# (prevention-over-detection — delete the class, do not patrol it).
*.tgz
**/*.tgz

# Generated type declarations — the publish pipeline emits these to a TEMP
# outDir + copies into staging (published-packages-ship-declarations), NEVER
# beside source. The emit pins declarationDir + an inline gate fails the
# publish if any .d.ts leaks into src/. This ignore is the last line of
# defense: even if a leak slipped both, a generated declaration can never be
# committed into the source tree.
src/**/*.d.ts
# Same leak, one directory over. The emit gate fails a publish only on a .d.ts
# in src/, and the rule above only covers src/ — so a declaration landing under
# test/ escapes BOTH, which is the identical shape as the generated/client/ gap
# recorded below. Measured 2026-08-01: 20 untracked .d.ts inside scala-guard's
# test/fixtures/**. Not cosmetic there — guard validators glob *.ts and .d.ts
# MATCHES that glob, so a stray declaration inside a clean/ or violating/
# fixture can silently change what that fixture asserts, in the very suite that
# proves the guards work. Safe to ignore blanket-wide: verified 0 tracked
# *.d.ts under test paths across scala-guard, scala-tools, codegen, scala-os,
# scala-db, scala-server, scala-primitives and scala-adapters, so this hides
# nothing anyone commits on purpose. The glob is test* rather than test because
# repos carry BOTH conventions side by side — scala-guard has test/fixtures/**
# AND tests/*.test.d.ts, and a rule matching only one leaves the other leaking,
# which is how this fix was initially shipped half-done.
test*/**/*.d.ts
# Prisma-generated client (generated by the repo's `db:generate` build script
# and by command-stamp codegen rows; output dir set by the
# schema generator's `output =`, e.g. db-core/generated/client/). A pure build
# artifact: regenerated on demand by codegen and re-emitted into the publish
# tarball from disk, so the source-repo copy is never committed. Lives OUTSIDE
# src/, so the src/**/*.d.ts rule above never reaches it — that gap was the
# 171-file scala-db + 96-file scala-apis churn class (2026-07-19).
**/generated/client/
# This ALSO covers hey-api's rest_client runtime at src/generated/client/, and
# that is deliberate: both are generated, both are imported at runtime, and
# NEITHER is committed. The publish stage re-emits both from disk
# (publish_stage.rs), which is the same contract the Prisma note above describes.
# Un-ignoring them instead would commit generated bytes and reopen the
# 171-file/96-file churn class this rule exists to close.
# Any package-root generated/ dir (a monorepo member's build artifacts:
# Prisma client, generated valibot schemas, etc.). Anchored to exactly one dir
# level (<pkg>/generated/) so it NEVER reaches src-nested tracked generated
# sources like db-core/src/registry/generated/ (registry-keys, statically
# imported — must stay committed). Closes db-validation/generated (323 untracked
# valibot schemas). Verified 2026-07-19: 0 tracked <dir>/generated/ workspace-wide.
# A FLATTENED package has no <pkg>/ level: its content IS the repo root, so the
# same one-level anchor now lands on src/generated/ — the tracked generated-source
# home the sentence above exists to protect, which silently un-tracks it (the
# files stay on disk, git add refuses them). The negation re-includes exactly
# that directory; the anchor still closes a real <pkg>/generated/, and in a
# wrapper layout the rule is inert because no root-level src/ exists there.
/*/generated/
!src/generated/
# Playwright failure artifacts (test-results appears only on failure; report
# when a reporter writes one) — structured-data UI tests (scala-os/ui.md).
test-results/
**/test-results/
playwright-report/
**/playwright-report/

# Cache + editor
.cache/
*.tsbuildinfo
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Bun's temp directory used during install / native-module builds (node-gyp scratch).
.bun-tmp/
**/.bun-tmp/

# Bun's package cache — never tracked.
.bun-cache/
**/.bun-cache/
**/\$HOME/

# Lockfiles — in package-source repos these are resolution caches managed
# by the package-poller and bun install, NOT deploy snapshots. The only
# committed bun.lock is in service repos where Railpack keys its install
# layer on it (railway-builds-resolve-fresh).
bun.lock
**/bun.lock

# scala-guard delta baseline
.scala-guard-baseline.json

# stage-0 bootstrap publisher staging dir
**/.stage0-pack/

.env
.env.*
!/.env.example

# Generated files materialising live secrets from registry secret rows.
# Tracked-in-repo would re-leak on every codegen run (infra-config incident
# 200c9d0->5298f95: a hand-edit added ecosystem.config.js here, a later
# `gitignore-publisher` regen OVERWROTE it because the template lacked the
# entry; a sync then `git add`-ed the now-un-ignored file back with plaintext
# secrets). Ignored at the TEMPLATE level so every regen preserves it.
# Regenerate locally per-host via: scala-tools codegen run --output ecosystem
ecosystem.config.js
**/scala-secrets.env*
# CLI agent config snapshots (claude-settings/codex-config/gemini-*/antigravity-*
# codegen outputs) — materialise SCALA_DEV_KEY from the secret row into the
# settings json; tracked-in-repo would leak the key. 1075b49 untracked them;
# this template entry makes the ignore survive `gitignore-publisher` regen.
server/home/.claude/
server/home/.codex/

# The staging tree a cli-startup AUDIENCE render places for another machine.
# It was written HERE because the staging root was a path literal compiled into
# the renderer, so 494 generated startup files came to be tracked in this repo
# and every regen re-leaked them (the tree the render owns, committed to the
# checkout it renders across). The staging root is a row
# (`machine_profile.audience_staging_root`) outside every checkout since
# 2026-10-02; this entry keeps the path ignored through every regen anyway, so
# the literal's residue can never be re-tracked by a sync. TEMPLATE level, for
# the reason the ecosystem.config.js entry above gives.
server/audiences/

# Vendor agent-skill scaffolding. The Prisma 8 CLI writes a skill tree into
# the directory it runs in (.claude/skills, .agents/skills, .cursor/skills,
# .devin/skills) — supplier docs, never source, and a skills tree inside a
# repo injects instructions into every agent session working there (measured
# 2026-09-29 01:10: four trees, 71 files each, written into infra-db-bun/
# db-core by a Prisma skill-install step and removed untracked). One tree of
# the same shape reached the infra-db-bun root through the retired-dev-host
# working-tree land (1502b1bf) before this entry existed. Ignored at the
# TEMPLATE level so every regen preserves it.
# The per-repo doc emission (repo_skills_dir on cli_routing): ONE directory
# per repo-owned doc, named `repos-<slug>`. The pattern is exactly that, never
# a bare `.opencode/` — a bare .opencode/ would also hide the repo's own
# hand-written files (opencode.json, hand-authored skills), and the emitted
# directory is the only thing that belongs here. TEMPLATE level, for the reason
# the ecosystem.config.js entry above gives: the first render is a commit
# storm across every checkout (commit_outputs commits every non-ignored
# generated path), so the ignore must be live BEFORE the first emission.
.opencode/skills/repos-*/
**/.claude/skills/
**/.agents/skills/
**/.cursor/skills/
**/.devin/skills/
